;; spec:   syntax §1.1, §3.16 (types §1.1)
;; expect: reject
;; error:  does not fit i8
;; A macro builds the Form (Int 300 :i8) and splices it into run-time
;; code. The Form enum lets a macro build it (syntax §3.16: "(Int v: i64
;; width: keyword)"), but no i8 value is 300:
;;   syntax §1.1: "A literal that does not fit its width (300i8,
;;   9223372036854775808) is a read error, never a wrap. Numbers carry
;;   their width in the reader; there is no literal polymorphism".
;; Written in source, (sext i64 300i8) is rejected with "integer literal
;; 300i8 does not fit i8 (-128 to 127)". Built by a macro it must be
;; rejected too (or at least mean what lIR's (i8 300) means); it cannot
;; be an i8 whose value is 300.
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   result: 300
;;   audit:  clean=true leak-cycles=0 leaks=0 errors=0
;; The macro's literal skips the reader's range check, and the
;; interpreter then carries an i8 holding 300 (sext of it is 300; (+ it
;; 0i8) would wrap it). A compiled program would get 44 from lIR's
;; truncation or an lIR verification error (method.md rules 6, 7). Also
;; accepted: (Flt 2.5 :f16), a float width that does not exist.
;; Diagnosis: expand (a Form spliced into code is not checked the way the
;; reader checks a literal); the spec says the check is the reader's and
;; is silent on macro-built literals.
(defmacro m () (List [(Sym "sext") (Sym "i64") (Int 300 :i8)]))

(defun main () -> i64 (m))
